 ## Definition
 
-For a finite-dimensional complex simple Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$ as in [[def-loop-algebra-of-a-simple-lie-algebra]], use the invariant form $B$ normalized by long roots of square $2$ in [[def-residue-two-cocycle-on-a-loop-algebra]]. By [[lem-adjoint-casimir-is-positive-scalar]], the Casimir $\Omega_B$ from [[def-casimir-operator-relative-to-an-invariant-form]] acts in the adjoint representation as $\gamma_B I$ for a unique positive real scalar $\gamma_B$. Define the **dual Coxeter number** by $h^\vee=\gamma_B/2$. Thus, for a $Q=-B|_{\mathfrak k}$-orthonormal basis of a compact real form from [[def-compact-real-form-of-a-complex-semisimple-lie-algebra]], $-\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(X_i)^2=2h^\vee I$; this is the normalization used in the affine central term and every Sugawara denominator below.
+For a finite-dimensional complex simple Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$ as in [[def-loop-algebra-of-a-simple-lie-algebra]], use the invariant form $B$ normalized by long roots of square $2$ in [[def-residue-two-cocycle-on-a-loop-algebra]]. The local trace calculation below shows that the Casimir $\Omega_B$ from [[def-casimir-operator-relative-to-an-invariant-form]] acts in the adjoint representation as $\gamma_B I$ for a unique positive real scalar $\gamma_B$. Define the **dual Coxeter number** by $h^\vee=\gamma_B/2$. If a compact real form $\mathfrak k$ from [[def-compact-real-form-of-a-complex-semisimple-lie-algebra]] is additionally supplied, then for every $Q=-B|_{\mathfrak k}$-orthonormal basis $(X_i)$ one has $-\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(X_i)^2=2h^\vee I$. This is the normalization used in the affine central term and every Sugawara denominator below; existence of a compact real form is not needed to define $h^\vee$.
 
 ## Facts & Assumptions
 
-**Given:** The normalized invariant form on a finite-dimensional complex simple Lie algebra and its compact real form.
+**Given:** A nonzero finite-dimensional complex simple Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$ and its normalized invariant form $B$. A compact real form is extra input only for step 4.1.
 
-[F1] [[def-compact-real-form-of-a-complex-semisimple-lie-algebra]]: the Killing form is negative definite on the compact real form.
+[F1] The normalization is $B=cK_{\mathfrak g}$ for a positive real scalar $c$; $B$ is nondegenerate, symmetric and invariant ([[def-residue-two-cocycle-on-a-loop-algebra]]). The Lie algebra is nonzero ([[def-loop-algebra-of-a-simple-lie-algebra]]).
 
-[F2] [[def-residue-two-cocycle-on-a-loop-algebra]]: $B$ is a positive-real rescaling of the Killing form, is invariant, and has long-root-square-$2$ normalization.
+[F2] For $B$-dual bases $x_i,x^i$, the adjoint Casimir operator is $C=\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(x_i)\operatorname{ad}(x^i)$ ([[def-casimir-operator-relative-to-an-invariant-form]]).
 
-[F3] [[def-casimir-operator-relative-to-an-invariant-form]]: for $B$-dual bases, $\Omega_B=\sum_i x_ix^i$.
+[F3] The Killing form is $K_{\mathfrak g}(x,y)=\operatorname{tr}_{\mathfrak g}(\operatorname{ad}(x)\operatorname{ad}(y))$ ([[def-killing-form-of-a-finite-dimensional-lie-algebra]]). The endomorphism trace is the sum of the diagonal entries in any finite basis ([[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]], [[def-trace-of-a-square-matrix]]).
 
-[F4] [[lem-adjoint-casimir-is-positive-scalar]]: the adjoint action of $\Omega_B$ is $\gamma_B I$ for a unique positive real scalar $\gamma_B$.
+[F4] A supplied compact real form has negative-definite restricted Killing form ([[def-compact-real-form-of-a-complex-semisimple-lie-algebra]]). A finite-dimensional positive-definite real inner-product space has an orthonormal basis by finite Gram–Schmidt ([[cor-finite-dimensional-inner-product-spaces-have-orthonormal-bases]]).
 
 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Set $Q=-B|_{\mathfrak k}$. By [F1] and [F2], $Q$ is positive definite. Choose a $Q$-orthonormal basis $(X_i)$ of $\mathfrak k$. Since $B=-Q$ on $\mathfrak k$, its $B$-dual basis is $(-X_i)$, and [F3] gives $\Omega_B=-\sum_iX_i^2$. [F1, F2, F3, choose]
+**Given:** The Lie algebra and normalized form above; write $N=\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathfrak g>0$.
 
-2.1 Applying the adjoint representation to step 1.1 gives $\operatorname{ad}(\Omega_B)=-\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(X_i)^2=\gamma_B I$ by [F4]. The definition $h^\vee=\gamma_B/2$ therefore yields $-\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(X_i)^2=2h^\vee I$, as stated. [F3, F4, step 1.1, given, algebra] ∎
+1.1 Fix one finite basis $x_1,\ldots,x_N$ and its $B$-dual basis $x^1,\ldots,x^N$, so $B(x_i,x^j)=\delta_i^j$. If $T x_i=\sum_j t_{ji}x_j$, then $B(Tx_i,x^i)=t_{ii}$ by duality. Therefore [F3] gives $\operatorname{tr}(T)=\sum_iB(Tx_i,x^i)$. All choices here are finite and $B$ is nondegenerate by [F1]. [F1, F3, choose, algebra]
+
+2.1 For arbitrary $x,y\in\mathfrak g$, [F2], symmetry and invariance give
+$$B(Cx,y)=\sum_iB([x_i,[x^i,x]],y)=\sum_iB([x^i,x],[y,x_i])=\sum_iB([x,[y,x_i]],x^i).$$
+Indeed the first conversion uses $B([a,b],c)=B(b,[c,a])$ with $a=x_i$, and the second uses the same identity with $a=x$, followed by symmetry. Step 1.1 identifies the final sum with $\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}(x)\operatorname{ad}(y))=K_{\mathfrak g}(x,y)$. Thus $B(Cx,y)=K_{\mathfrak g}(x,y)$, with no compact real form or spectral assertion. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 By [F1], $K_{\mathfrak g}(x,y)=c^{-1}B(x,y)$. Step 2.1 implies $B(Cx-c^{-1}x,y)=0$ for every $y$, so nondegeneracy yields $Cx=c^{-1}x$ for every $x$. Hence $C=\gamma_B I$ with $\gamma_B=c^{-1}>0$. The scalar is unique because $\mathfrak g\ne0$. Its half $h^\vee=\gamma_B/2$ is therefore well defined and positive without a compact-form existence theorem or a choice axiom. [F1, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 If a compact real form $\mathfrak k$ is supplied, [F1] and [F4] make $Q=-B|_{\mathfrak k}$ positive definite. Take a finite $Q$-orthonormal real basis $(X_i)$ by [F4]; it is also a complex basis of $\mathfrak g$ because $\mathfrak k$ is a real form. Its $B$-dual basis is $(-X_i)$, since $B(X_i,-X_j)=\delta_{ij}$. Thus [F2] gives $C=-\sum_i\operatorname{ad}(X_i)^2$, and step 3.1 identifies this with $2h^\vee I$. This proves the conditional compact-basis formula without constructing a compact real form or inferring its existence from its definition. [F1, F2, F4, step 3.1, choose, algebra] ∎
